-- ID 1026: Evaluate whether a parent should structure a liquidation under Section 332 or Section 331
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Wexford Industries, a C corporation, owns 85% of Greystone Manufacturing. Greystone has $2,400,000 in appreciated assets (basis $900,000) and $600,000 of suspended net operating losses. A tax advisor is evaluating whether Wexford should liquidate Greystone under Section 332 (subsidiary liquidation) or first reduce its ownership below 80% and liquidate under Section 331 (general liquidation). Under Section 332, Greystone recognizes no gain and Wexford takes a carryover basis in the assets, but the NOLs transfer to Wexford subject to Section 382 limitations. Under Section 331, Greystone recognizes gain on the appreciated assets but Wexford receives a stepped-up fair market value basis. Evaluate which liquidation structure is more advantageous for Wexford.',
  choices = '["Section 332 is always preferable because avoiding gain recognition at the subsidiary level outweighs any benefit from a stepped-up basis in the assets", "Section 331 is preferable because the gain recognized by Greystone is offset by its $600,000 NOLs, reducing the net taxable gain to $900,000 while Wexford receives a $2,400,000 stepped-up basis — the higher basis produces greater future depreciation deductions and lower gain on eventual asset sales, which over time exceeds the one-time tax cost", "Section 332 is preferable because the $600,000 NOLs carry over to Wexford without limitation and can be used immediately against Wexford''s other income", "The two approaches produce identical after-tax results because the stepped-up basis benefit under Section 331 exactly offsets the gain recognition cost in all cases"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): This requires evaluating the trade-offs between two valid liquidation structures. Under Section 332, no gain is recognized at the subsidiary level, and Wexford takes a carryover basis of $900,000 in the assets. The NOLs transfer but are subject to Section 382 annual limitations, reducing their immediate utility. Under Section 331, Greystone recognizes a $1,500,000 gain ($2,400,000 minus $900,000), but the $600,000 of NOLs offset this, producing a net taxable gain of only $900,000 at Greystone. Meanwhile, Wexford receives a stepped-up basis of $2,400,000 in the assets. The additional $1,500,000 of basis produces significantly greater future depreciation deductions and lower gains on asset dispositions — a benefit that compounds over time and typically exceeds the one-time tax on $900,000 of net gain. (A) is wrong because it ignores the long-term value of the stepped-up basis. (C) is wrong because the NOLs are subject to Section 382 limitations after a subsidiary liquidation, not available without restriction. (D) is wrong because the outcomes differ depending on the asset appreciation, NOL availability, and future holding period.',
  correct_index = 1,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1026;

-- ID 1027: Recommend liquidation timing strategy to minimize tax impact
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is counseling Hargrove Capital, a closely held C corporation with three equal shareholders. The corporation holds $3,000,000 in assets (basis $1,800,000) and $400,000 of accumulated earnings and profits. One shareholder, Prescott, has a stock basis of $200,000, while the other two shareholders each have a basis of $500,000. Prescott is terminally ill and his estate would receive a stepped-up basis under Section 1014. The CPA is considering whether to liquidate the corporation now or delay liquidation until after Prescott''s passing. Recommend the most appropriate timing strategy to minimize the combined tax burden on all shareholders.',
  choices = '["Delay liquidation until after Prescott''s death so his estate receives a stepped-up basis in the stock, eliminating Prescott''s $800,000 built-in gain ($1,000,000 FMV share minus $200,000 basis) — the two remaining shareholders face the same gain regardless of timing, so the delay produces a net tax savings equal to the tax on Prescott''s $800,000 of avoided gain", "Liquidate immediately because delaying creates accumulated earnings tax exposure under Section 531 that will exceed any basis step-up benefit", "Liquidate now because Section 1014 does not apply to corporate stock received in a liquidation and the stepped-up basis benefit is illusory", "The timing is irrelevant because all shareholders will pay the same total tax whether the corporation liquidates before or after Prescott''s death"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Evaluating the timing requires comparing total tax under each scenario. If the corporation liquidates now, all three shareholders recognize gain: Prescott recognizes $800,000 ($1,000,000 FMV share minus $200,000 basis), and the other two each recognize $500,000 ($1,000,000 minus $500,000). If liquidation is delayed until after Prescott''s death, his estate receives a stepped-up basis of $1,000,000 under Section 1014, eliminating Prescott''s $800,000 gain entirely. The other two shareholders'' gains remain $500,000 each regardless of timing. The net benefit of delay is the tax saved on $800,000 of gain. The corporation-level gain on distributing appreciated assets ($1,200,000) occurs in either scenario and is unaffected by timing. (B) is wrong because the accumulated earnings tax risk can be managed with proper documentation of business purpose and does not automatically exceed the stepped-up basis benefit. (C) is wrong because Section 1014 applies to the stock itself — the estate receives a stepped-up basis in the corporate stock, which reduces gain on the liquidating distribution. (D) is wrong because the timing change eliminates Prescott''s shareholder-level gain.',
  correct_index = 0,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1027;

-- ID 1028: Assess whether contributing property with built-in gain under 351 is advisable given boot received
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is counseling Thornfield, who plans to contribute equipment (basis $50,000, FMV $300,000) to a newly formed corporation in exchange for 80% of the stock and $75,000 cash (boot). The corporation will use the equipment in operations and expects to depreciate it over seven years. Alternatively, Thornfield could sell the equipment outright for $300,000, pay tax on the full $250,000 gain, and contribute cash to the corporation. Assess whether the Section 351 contribution with boot is advisable compared to the sell-then-contribute alternative.',
  choices = '["The Section 351 contribution with boot is clearly superior because Thornfield recognizes only $75,000 of the $250,000 gain now and defers $175,000, preserving more after-tax capital than selling outright", "Selling outright is preferable because it gives the corporation a full $300,000 basis in the equipment, generating substantially higher depreciation deductions that benefit Thornfield as the 80% shareholder — the increased depreciation over seven years can offset or exceed the additional tax from recognizing the full $250,000 gain upfront", "The two approaches produce identical after-tax results because the gain deferred under Section 351 is simply recaptured through lower depreciation deductions at the corporate level", "The Section 351 contribution is inadvisable because receiving boot disqualifies the entire transaction from nonrecognition treatment, triggering the full $250,000 gain regardless"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under Section 351(b), Thornfield recognizes gain equal to the lesser of boot received ($75,000) or realized gain ($250,000), so only $75,000 is recognized. Thornfield''s basis in the stock is $50,000 (property basis) minus $75,000 (boot) plus $75,000 (gain recognized) = $50,000. The corporation''s basis in the equipment is $50,000 (transferor basis) plus $75,000 (gain recognized) = $125,000 under Section 362. Under the sell-then-contribute alternative, Thornfield recognizes the full $250,000 gain and the corporation gets a $300,000 basis, producing $175,000 more in depreciation deductions over seven years. At a 21% corporate rate, that extra depreciation saves approximately $36,750 in corporate taxes over seven years, but Thornfield pays tax on an additional $175,000 of gain immediately — at capital gains rates of 20%, that is $35,000 in additional current tax. The deferral of $175,000 of gain under Section 351 provides a time-value benefit that generally exceeds the incrementally lower depreciation. (B) overstates the depreciation benefit relative to the immediate tax cost and ignores the time value of deferral. (C) is wrong because the timing difference creates real economic value. (D) is wrong because boot does not disqualify the exchange — it triggers partial recognition only.',
  correct_index = 0,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1028;

