-- ID 913: Evaluate whether restructuring participation can maximize loss deductions
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is evaluating the tax position of Dr. Langford, a surgeon with $450,000 of W-2 income, who owns a rental duplex generating $40,000 in annual losses. Dr. Langford currently spends 200 hours per year managing the property. Because her AGI exceeds $150,000, the $25,000 active participation rental loss allowance is fully phased out. A colleague suggests Dr. Langford could restructure her activities to qualify as a real estate professional under Section 469(c)(7) by reducing her surgical practice to part-time and increasing rental management hours. Evaluate whether this restructuring strategy is advisable to maximize loss deductions.',
  choices = '["The strategy is inadvisable because the income reduction from going part-time likely exceeds the tax benefit of deducting $40,000 in rental losses — Dr. Langford would need to spend over 750 hours in real property trades and make it more than half her total service hours, requiring a dramatic reduction in surgical income that produces a net economic loss far greater than the tax savings", "The strategy is advisable because qualifying as a real estate professional automatically converts all rental losses to nonpassive, allowing the full $40,000 deduction against remaining W-2 income", "The strategy is unnecessary because Dr. Langford already qualifies as a real estate professional since she actively participates in the rental activity", "The strategy is advisable only if Dr. Langford materializes the losses by selling the property, because real estate professional status does not affect suspended passive losses from prior years"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Evaluating this strategy requires weighing the tax benefit against the economic cost. To qualify as a real estate professional, Dr. Langford must spend more than 750 hours in real property trades or businesses and those hours must constitute more than half of her total personal service hours. Currently spending 200 hours on the rental, she would need to increase to over 750 hours and simultaneously reduce her surgical hours so that real estate hours exceed 50% of total. If she currently works 2,000 surgical hours, she would need to reduce them below 750 to make real estate the majority — a dramatic income reduction. The tax benefit of deducting $40,000 at her marginal rate (approximately $14,800 at 37%) is far less than the income lost from cutting surgical hours by more than half. (B) is technically correct about the tax treatment but ignores the economic cost of qualifying. (C) is wrong because active participation is distinct from real estate professional status — the former has a $25,000 cap that phases out at high AGI, while the latter requires the 750-hour and majority-of-services tests. (D) is wrong because real estate professional status recharacterizes current-year rental losses as nonpassive.',
  correct_index = 0,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 913;

-- ID 914: Recommend disposition strategy to release maximum suspended passive losses
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is counseling Mercer, who has $180,000 of suspended passive losses from a limited partnership interest. Mercer''s basis in the partnership is $250,000 and the FMV is $200,000. Mercer is considering three strategies: (1) sell the entire interest to an unrelated buyer for $200,000, (2) gift the interest to his adult daughter, or (3) sell 60% of the interest and retain 40%. Recommend which disposition strategy releases the maximum suspended passive losses in the current year.',
  choices = '["Gifting to his daughter is best because the suspended losses transfer to her and she can deduct them against her own income immediately", "Selling 60% is optimal because it releases a proportional 60% of suspended losses ($108,000) while preserving the remaining 40% interest for future income that can absorb the rest", "Selling the entire interest to an unrelated buyer is the best strategy because a fully taxable disposition to an unrelated party under Section 469(g) releases all $180,000 of suspended passive losses in the current year — a gift does not trigger release and a partial sale releases only the proportionate share", "Retaining the interest is preferable because Mercer can offset the suspended losses against future passive income from other investments without triggering the $50,000 built-in loss on disposition"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under IRC Section 469(g)(1), when a taxpayer disposes of an entire interest in a passive activity in a fully taxable transaction to an unrelated party, all suspended passive losses are released and deductible against nonpassive income. Selling 100% of the interest to an unrelated buyer releases all $180,000 of suspended losses. In contrast, a gift under Section 469(g)(2) does not release suspended losses — instead, the losses increase the donee''s basis in the interest but are never deductible by either party as a current loss. A partial sale of 60% releases only the proportionate suspended losses ($108,000), leaving $72,000 suspended. Since Mercer''s goal is maximum current-year loss release, the complete sale is clearly superior. The $50,000 economic loss on the sale ($200,000 FMV minus $250,000 basis) produces a capital loss that is also deductible, providing additional tax benefit. (A) is wrong because gifts do not release suspended losses for deduction. (B) is correct about proportionality but releases $72,000 less than the full sale. (D) sacrifices immediate deduction for uncertain future passive income.',
  correct_index = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 914;

-- ID 915: Assess whether a taxpayer should increase at-risk amount to unlock loss deductions
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is assessing the position of Hartwell, who has $120,000 of suspended losses from a real estate partnership due to at-risk limitations under Section 465. Hartwell''s current at-risk amount is $0 because prior losses consumed his original $200,000 cash contribution and his share of recourse debt. The partnership has $500,000 of nonrecourse debt secured by the property. Hartwell is considering contributing an additional $120,000 cash to the partnership to unlock the suspended losses. His marginal tax rate is 37%. Assess whether this strategy is advisable.',
  choices = '["The contribution is not advisable because the $120,000 cash outlay to unlock $120,000 of losses produces a tax savings of only $44,400 (37% of $120,000) — Hartwell spends $120,000 to save $44,400, a net cash loss of $75,600, and the real estate''s nonrecourse financing already provides basis for loss deductions without additional cash investment", "The contribution is advisable because it produces an immediate $120,000 deduction that reduces Hartwell''s current-year tax bill by $44,400 and the cash remains invested in the partnership generating future returns", "The contribution is unnecessary because qualified nonrecourse financing on real estate is included in the at-risk amount under Section 465(b)(6), meaning Hartwell''s $500,000 share of nonrecourse debt already provides sufficient at-risk basis to deduct the suspended losses", "The contribution is inadvisable because additional contributions to a partnership with a zero at-risk amount are treated as returns of capital rather than increases to the at-risk basis"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under IRC Section 465(b)(6), qualified nonrecourse financing secured by real property used in an activity of holding real property is included in the taxpayer''s at-risk amount. This is a critical exception to the general rule that nonrecourse debt is excluded from at-risk calculations. If the partnership''s $500,000 nonrecourse debt is qualified nonrecourse financing (from a bank or lending institution, not the seller or a related party), Hartwell''s share of that debt increases his at-risk amount, potentially unlocking the suspended losses without any additional cash contribution. The CPA should first verify whether the financing qualifies under Section 465(b)(6) before recommending a $120,000 cash outlay. (A) correctly identifies the unfavorable economics of the cash contribution but misses the qualified nonrecourse financing exception. (B) ignores that the cash contribution may be unnecessary. (D) is wrong because additional contributions do increase the at-risk amount under Section 465.',
  correct_index = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 915;

