-- ID 968: Evaluate whether credit or deduction election is more beneficial
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is evaluating the foreign tax position of Ashford Global Corp, a domestic C corporation that paid $800,000 in foreign taxes on $3,200,000 of foreign-source income. Ashford''s worldwide taxable income is $10,000,000 and its U.S. tax before credits is $2,100,000. The foreign tax credit limitation is $672,000 (21% of $3,200,000). Ashford is considering whether to claim the $800,000 as a credit (limited to $672,000 with $128,000 excess carried forward) or deduct the full $800,000 against taxable income. Evaluate which election is more beneficial for the current year.',
  choices = '["The deduction is more beneficial because it reduces taxable income by $800,000, saving $168,000 in tax (21% of $800,000), whereas the credit only offsets $672,000 of tax liability", "The credit is more beneficial even though limited to $672,000 — a dollar-for-dollar reduction in tax liability of $672,000 plus a $128,000 carryforward exceeds the $168,000 tax savings from a deduction, making the credit preferable by $504,000 in the current year alone, with additional future value from the carryforward", "The two methods produce identical tax results because the limitation mechanism ensures credits and deductions converge to the same effective rate on foreign income", "The deduction is preferable because the excess credit carryforward expires after one year and cannot be used against future high-tax foreign income"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): A foreign tax credit is a dollar-for-dollar reduction of U.S. tax liability, while a deduction merely reduces taxable income. The credit, even limited to $672,000, directly offsets $672,000 of the $2,100,000 U.S. tax. The deduction of $800,000 reduces taxable income from $10,000,000 to $9,200,000, saving only $168,000 (21% of $800,000). The credit saves $672,000 versus the deduction''s $168,000 — a $504,000 advantage for the credit. Additionally, the $128,000 excess credit carries forward for 10 years under Section 904(c), providing future tax offsets. Even when limited, the foreign tax credit almost always dominates the deduction election because credits offset tax while deductions offset income. (A) is wrong because it compares the deduction''s $168,000 savings against the credit limitation of $672,000, which is itself a larger number. (C) is wrong because credits and deductions have fundamentally different mechanics. (D) is wrong because excess foreign tax credits carry forward for 10 years, not one.',
  correct_index = 1,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 968;

-- ID 969: Recommend entity structure to minimize GILTI exposure
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is advising Ridgemont Enterprises, which currently operates a foreign subsidiary (CFC) through a domestic C corporation parent. The CFC earns $5,000,000 of tested income with $8,000,000 of QBAI. The C corporation parent claims the 50% Section 250 deduction on GILTI. Ridgemont is considering restructuring so that the CFC is owned directly by the individual shareholders instead of the C corporation. Recommend whether the current C corporation parent structure or direct individual ownership better minimizes GILTI tax exposure.',
  choices = '["The C corporation parent structure is clearly superior — the 10% deemed return on $8,000,000 QBAI ($800,000) reduces GILTI inclusion to $4,200,000, and the 50% Section 250 deduction further reduces the taxable amount to $2,100,000, producing an effective tax rate of approximately 10.5% on the GILTI inclusion — individual shareholders receive no Section 250 deduction, so the full $4,200,000 GILTI inclusion would be taxed at ordinary rates up to 37%, producing a dramatically higher tax burden", "Direct individual ownership is preferable because individuals are not subject to GILTI inclusion under Section 951A, eliminating the tax entirely", "Individual ownership is preferable because GILTI is taxed at the qualified dividend rate of 20% for individuals, compared to the 21% corporate rate", "The structures produce identical tax results because Section 250 applies equally to corporate and individual shareholders"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Evaluating the two structures requires comparing effective tax rates on the GILTI inclusion. Under the C corporation parent: GILTI tested income of $5,000,000 minus the deemed tangible income return of $800,000 (10% of $8,000,000 QBAI) equals $4,200,000 net GILTI. The 50% Section 250 deduction reduces the taxable portion to $2,100,000, taxed at 21% corporate rate for $441,000 — an effective rate of approximately 10.5% on the GILTI inclusion. Under direct individual ownership: the same $4,200,000 GILTI inclusion is taxed at ordinary income rates (up to 37%) with no Section 250 deduction available to individuals. At the top rate, the tax would be approximately $1,554,000 — more than three times the corporate structure''s tax. (B) is wrong because individuals who are U.S. shareholders of CFCs are subject to GILTI inclusion under Section 951A. (C) is wrong because GILTI is taxed as ordinary income, not as qualified dividends. (D) is wrong because the Section 250 deduction is available only to domestic C corporations.',
  correct_index = 0,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 969;

-- ID 970: Assess whether restructuring ownership below 10% threshold is advisable
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is assessing whether Thornfield Holdings should restructure its ownership in a foreign corporation to avoid CFC reporting obligations. Thornfield currently owns 12% of the foreign corporation, which qualifies as a CFC because other U.S. shareholders collectively own over 50%. The foreign corporation earns $2,000,000 of Subpart F income annually. If Thornfield reduces its ownership to 9%, it would no longer be a ''U.S. shareholder'' under Section 951(b) (which requires 10% ownership). However, the restructuring would require selling 3% to an unrelated foreign buyer at a $150,000 loss. Assess whether this ownership restructuring is advisable.',
  choices = '["The restructuring is advisable because avoiding U.S. shareholder status eliminates Thornfield''s annual Subpart F inclusion of $240,000 (12% of $2,000,000), producing ongoing tax savings that quickly exceed the one-time $150,000 loss on the share sale", "The restructuring is inadvisable because the constructive ownership rules under Section 318 (as modified by Section 958) will attribute the sold shares back to Thornfield, maintaining its U.S. shareholder status regardless of the sale", "The restructuring is unnecessary because Section 951(b) defines a U.S. shareholder as owning 25% or more, so Thornfield''s 12% ownership does not trigger CFC inclusions in the first place", "The restructuring is advisable only if Thornfield also renounces its voting rights, because the 10% threshold is measured by voting power alone and not by value"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under IRC Section 951(b), a U.S. shareholder is defined as a U.S. person who owns 10% or more of the total combined voting power or 10% or more of the total value of shares. At 12%, Thornfield is a U.S. shareholder and must include its pro rata share of Subpart F income — $240,000 annually (12% of $2,000,000). If Thornfield reduces ownership to 9% by selling to an unrelated foreign buyer, it falls below the 10% threshold and is no longer a U.S. shareholder, eliminating the annual Subpart F inclusion. The $150,000 loss on the sale is a one-time cost, while the tax savings from eliminating $240,000 of annual income inclusion (approximately $88,800 per year at a 37% rate) recoup the loss within two years. The sale to an unrelated foreign person avoids constructive ownership attribution. (B) is wrong because selling to an unrelated foreign buyer does not trigger constructive ownership attribution back to Thornfield. (C) is wrong because the threshold is 10%, not 25%. (D) is wrong because after the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act, the 10% threshold applies to both voting power and value.',
  correct_index = 0,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 970;

