-- ID 926: Evaluate whether gifting or bequeathing appreciated property minimizes total tax
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is evaluating wealth transfer strategies for Eleanor Prescott, age 78, who owns publicly traded stock with a basis of $100,000 and FMV of $900,000. Eleanor wants to transfer the stock to her grandson. Option A is to gift the stock now, giving the grandson a carryover basis of $100,000. Option B is to hold the stock until death, giving the grandson a stepped-up basis of $900,000 (assuming FMV remains stable) through a bequest. The grandson plans to sell the stock within one year of receiving it. Eleanor''s estate is well below the federal estate tax exemption. Evaluate which strategy minimizes the total family tax burden.',
  choices = '["Gifting now is preferable because it removes future appreciation from Eleanor''s estate and the grandson can use the annual gift tax exclusion to avoid any transfer tax", "Gifting is preferable because Eleanor can claim a charitable deduction for the appreciated value of the stock transferred to her grandson", "The two strategies are equivalent because the gift tax exclusion eliminates transfer tax on the gift just as the estate exemption eliminates tax on the bequest", "Bequeathing is clearly preferable — the grandson inherits a stepped-up basis of $900,000 under Section 1014 and pays zero capital gains tax on a sale at $900,000, whereas a gift results in $800,000 of taxable gain when the grandson sells, producing approximately $160,000 or more in capital gains tax — since Eleanor''s estate is below the exemption, the bequest triggers no estate tax, making the stepped-up basis a pure tax savings"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): When comparing a lifetime gift versus a bequest, the key variable is the income tax impact of the basis rules. A gift carries over the donor''s basis under Section 1015 — the grandson receives a $100,000 basis and recognizes $800,000 of capital gain upon sale, producing approximately $160,000 in federal tax at the 20% long-term rate (potentially more with the 3.8% NIIT). A bequest provides a stepped-up basis under Section 1014 equal to the FMV at death ($900,000), eliminating the entire built-in gain. Since Eleanor''s estate is below the exemption threshold, no estate tax is due on the bequest. The total family tax savings from the bequest strategy is approximately $160,000 or more — the full capital gains tax avoided. (A) is wrong because removing appreciation from the estate provides no benefit when the estate is already below the exemption. (B) is wrong because transfers to a grandson are not charitable contributions. (C) is wrong because the strategies are not equivalent — the income tax treatment differs by $160,000+.',
  correct_index = 3,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 926;

-- ID 927: Recommend timing strategy for gifting property with built-in loss
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is counseling Margaret Chen, who owns investment property with a basis of $500,000 and FMV of $300,000. Margaret wants to transfer the property to her daughter, who would hold it as an investment. Under Section 1015(a), if Margaret gifts the property, the daughter''s basis for determining loss is limited to the $300,000 FMV at the time of the gift — the $200,000 built-in loss disappears for both parties. Alternatively, Margaret could sell the property, recognize the $200,000 loss herself, and gift the $300,000 cash proceeds to her daughter. Recommend the most appropriate strategy.',
  choices = '["Gift the property directly because the daughter will eventually sell it and recognize the $200,000 loss using the carryover basis of $500,000", "Margaret should sell the property first, recognize the $200,000 capital loss on her own return, and then gift the $300,000 cash proceeds to her daughter — this preserves the economic value of the loss deduction that would be permanently destroyed by a direct gift under the dual-basis rules of Section 1015", "Gift the property directly because the built-in loss transfers to the daughter and can offset her investment gains in a lower tax bracket", "Neither strategy matters because the $200,000 loss is a paper loss that has no real tax value under either approach"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under Section 1015(a), when gifted property has a FMV below the donor''s basis, the donee''s basis for determining loss is limited to FMV ($300,000). The $200,000 built-in loss is permanently destroyed — neither Margaret nor her daughter can ever deduct it. By contrast, if Margaret sells the property before gifting, she recognizes the $200,000 capital loss on her own return, which can offset capital gains or up to $3,000 of ordinary income annually with indefinite carryforward. She then gifts $300,000 cash, which has no basis issues. The sell-then-gift strategy preserves $200,000 of tax-deductible loss that would otherwise vanish. (A) is wrong because the dual-basis rule of Section 1015 prevents the daughter from using the $500,000 basis to compute a loss. (C) is wrong for the same reason — the built-in loss does not transfer to the donee. (D) is wrong because the $200,000 loss has real tax value when recognized by Margaret through a sale.',
  correct_index = 1,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 927;

-- ID 929: Assess whether a client should hold appreciated assets until death vs. selling now
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is assessing the position of Robert Ashworth, age 72, who holds a concentrated stock position with a basis of $200,000 and current FMV of $1,400,000. Robert needs $500,000 of liquidity for medical expenses. He is considering selling enough shares to raise $500,000 (recognizing approximately $171,000 in capital gains) versus borrowing $500,000 against the portfolio and holding all shares until death for a stepped-up basis. Robert''s life expectancy is 10 years and the loan interest rate is 6%. Assess whether the borrow-and-hold strategy is advisable.',
  choices = '["Selling is always preferable because the certainty of a known tax liability outweighs the speculative benefit of a future stepped-up basis", "Borrowing against the portfolio is inadvisable because loan proceeds are taxable income, making the strategy no better than selling shares directly", "The borrow-and-hold strategy is only advisable if Robert itemizes deductions, because investment interest expense is the sole benefit of borrowing", "The borrow-and-hold strategy is advisable because the total loan interest over Robert''s expected life ($300,000 at 6% simple interest on $500,000) is less than the capital gains tax avoided on the full $1,200,000 built-in gain at death — the stepped-up basis eliminates approximately $285,600 in tax (assuming 23.8% combined rate), producing a net benefit even after interest costs, provided the portfolio appreciates enough to service the debt"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Evaluating this strategy requires comparing the cost of borrowing against the tax savings from the stepped-up basis. If Robert sells $500,000 of shares, he recognizes approximately $171,000 of long-term capital gain (basis proportional to the sale), generating roughly $40,700 in tax at the 23.8% combined federal rate. If he borrows $500,000 instead and holds all shares until death, the total interest cost over 10 years is approximately $300,000 (at 6%). However, the stepped-up basis under Section 1014 eliminates tax on the entire $1,200,000 built-in gain — a savings of approximately $285,600. Even after accounting for the $300,000 in interest (partially deductible as investment interest), the net position depends on portfolio growth, actual life span, and interest deductibility. The strategy is generally advisable because loan proceeds are not taxable income, the interest may be deductible, and the stepped-up basis benefit compounds as the stock appreciates further. (A) is wrong because it dismisses a quantifiable tax benefit without analysis. (B) is wrong because loan proceeds are not taxable income. (C) is wrong because the primary benefit is basis step-up avoidance of capital gains tax, not the interest deduction.',
  correct_index = 3,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 929;

