-- ID 941: Evaluate whether backdoor Roth strategy is more beneficial than traditional IRA
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is evaluating retirement strategies for Dr. Caldwell, age 45, who earns $350,000 annually and is ineligible for direct Roth IRA contributions due to the income phaseout. Dr. Caldwell has no existing traditional IRA balances. The advisor is considering a backdoor Roth strategy (contribute $7,000 to a nondeductible traditional IRA, then immediately convert to Roth) versus investing $7,000 in a taxable brokerage account. Dr. Caldwell expects to be in a similar or higher tax bracket at retirement in 20 years. Evaluate which strategy is more beneficial.',
  choices = '["The taxable brokerage account is preferable because it provides more flexibility and the long-term capital gains rate at withdrawal is lower than ordinary income rates on Roth conversions", "The backdoor Roth is inadvisable because the IRS has ruled that the step-transaction doctrine invalidates backdoor Roth conversions for high-income taxpayers", "The two strategies produce identical after-tax results because the Roth conversion is taxed at ordinary rates, which offsets the benefit of tax-free withdrawals", "The backdoor Roth is clearly more beneficial — since Dr. Caldwell has no existing traditional IRA balances, the pro-rata rule under Section 408(d)(2) does not apply and the conversion is essentially tax-free on the $7,000 basis amount, providing 20 years of tax-free growth and tax-free qualified distributions versus annual taxation of dividends and gains in the brokerage account"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): The backdoor Roth strategy works optimally when the taxpayer has no existing pre-tax traditional IRA balances. Under Section 408(d)(2), conversions from traditional to Roth IRAs are subject to the pro-rata rule, which taxes conversions proportionally based on the ratio of pre-tax to after-tax amounts across all traditional IRAs. With no pre-tax balances, Dr. Caldwell''s $7,000 nondeductible contribution has a full $7,000 basis, so the conversion triggers zero tax (or minimal tax on any growth between contribution and conversion). Once in the Roth, the funds grow tax-free for 20 years and qualified distributions are entirely tax-free. In a taxable account, dividends are taxed annually, and gains are taxed upon sale (even at favorable capital gains rates). Over 20 years, the compounding advantage of tax-free growth in the Roth significantly outperforms the taxable account. (A) is wrong because the Roth''s tax-free growth over 20 years exceeds the benefit of lower capital gains rates in a taxable account. (B) is wrong because Congress explicitly allowed backdoor Roth conversions and the IRS has not invalidated the strategy. (C) is wrong because the conversion is nearly tax-free given the nondeductible basis.',
  correct_index = 3,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 941;

-- ID 942: Recommend retirement plan type based on business owner's age and income profile
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is advising Thornton, a 58-year-old sole proprietor with $400,000 of net self-employment income and no employees. Thornton wants to maximize retirement contributions over the next 7 years before retirement at age 65. The CPA is comparing three options: (1) a SEP-IRA with a maximum contribution of approximately $66,000, (2) a solo 401(k) with employee deferrals of $30,500 (including catch-up) plus an employer contribution up to 25% of compensation, and (3) a defined benefit plan that could allow annual contributions exceeding $200,000 based on actuarial calculations. Recommend the most appropriate plan type for Thornton''s situation.',
  choices = '["The SEP-IRA is best because it has the lowest administrative burden and the $66,000 contribution limit is sufficient for most retirement planning needs", "The solo 401(k) is optimal because combining employee deferrals with employer contributions maximizes the total contribution while maintaining moderate administrative costs", "The defined benefit plan best achieves Thornton''s goal — at age 58 with only 7 years to fund retirement, the actuarial calculation for a defined benefit plan allows annual contributions well above $200,000 because the short funding horizon requires accelerated contributions to meet the promised benefit, far exceeding the contribution limits of either the SEP-IRA or solo 401(k)", "All three plans produce identical retirement outcomes because the total tax deduction over 7 years converges to the same amount regardless of annual contribution limits"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): For a high-income business owner approaching retirement age with no employees, the defined benefit plan is uniquely advantageous. The contribution limit is based on an actuarial calculation of the amount needed to fund a defined retirement benefit (up to $275,000 annually in 2024, indexed for inflation). Because Thornton is 58 with only 7 years to fund the benefit, the required annual contribution is substantially higher than defined contribution plan limits — often exceeding $200,000 per year. This allows Thornton to shelter far more income from current taxation than either the SEP-IRA (capped at approximately $66,000) or the solo 401(k) (capped at approximately $73,500 with catch-up contributions). The administrative costs and actuarial filing requirements are higher, but at $400,000 of income with a 7-year horizon, the additional tax deductions of $130,000+ per year vastly outweigh the administrative costs. (A) is wrong because the SEP limit is far below what Thornton could contribute through a defined benefit plan. (B) is wrong for the same reason — the solo 401(k) is better than a SEP but still far below the defined benefit plan. (D) is wrong because annual contribution limits directly determine total retirement savings.',
  correct_index = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 942;

-- ID 944: Assess whether 529 distribution strategy or education credit produces greater benefit
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is assessing options for the Hargrove family. Their daughter''s college tuition is $15,000 for the year, and they have $80,000 in a 529 plan. The family qualifies for the American Opportunity Tax Credit (AOTC), which provides up to $2,500 per year for the first $4,000 of qualified expenses. The advisor is deciding between (1) using the 529 to pay the full $15,000 tuition or (2) paying $4,000 out of pocket to claim the AOTC and using the 529 for only $11,000. Assess which distribution strategy produces the greater tax benefit.',
  choices = '["Using the 529 for the full $15,000 is better because it avoids out-of-pocket spending and the tax-free distribution benefit exceeds the AOTC value", "The two strategies are equivalent because 529 distributions and education credits can be claimed on the same expenses", "Paying $4,000 out of pocket and claiming the AOTC is clearly better — the AOTC provides up to $2,500 in credits (100% of the first $2,000 plus 25% of the next $2,000), of which $1,000 is refundable, producing a direct tax reduction that exceeds the tax benefit of a $4,000 tax-free 529 distribution, while the remaining $11,000 of tuition is still covered tax-free by the 529", "The full 529 distribution is preferable because claiming the AOTC on expenses also paid by a 529 produces no penalty"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): This requires evaluating the coordination rules between 529 plans and education credits. Under Section 529(c)(3)(B)(v), expenses used to claim an education credit cannot also be treated as qualified 529 expenses. The advisor must allocate: $4,000 of tuition supports the AOTC claim, and $11,000 is paid from the 529 tax-free. The AOTC on $4,000 of expenses produces $2,500 in credits ($2,000 x 100% + $2,000 x 25%), of which $1,000 is refundable. The alternative — using the 529 for $4,000 instead — merely provides a tax-free distribution worth the taxes that would have been owed on the earnings portion of that $4,000 (a much smaller benefit, typically a few hundred dollars depending on the earnings ratio). The $2,500 credit far exceeds the tax-free growth benefit on $4,000 of 529 distributions. (A) is wrong because the AOTC''s value dramatically exceeds the incremental 529 benefit on those $4,000 of expenses. (B) is wrong because the same expenses cannot support both benefits simultaneously. (D) is wrong because using 529 distributions for AOTC-eligible expenses without coordination wastes the credit opportunity.',
  correct_index = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 944;

