-- ID 1010: Evaluate whether timing distributions before or after year-end reduces overall trust tax burden
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is evaluating the Ashworth Family Trust, a complex trust with $200,000 of distributable net income (DNI) for the current year. The trust is in the 37% bracket (income over $15,200 for trusts). The sole beneficiary, James, has taxable income of $60,000 and is in the 22% bracket. The trustee has discretion over distribution timing. The CPA is evaluating whether the trustee should distribute the full $200,000 before year-end or retain it in the trust. Evaluate which approach minimizes the combined tax burden on the trust and beneficiary.',
  choices = '["Retaining the income in the trust is preferable because trusts pay a lower effective tax rate than individuals on investment income", "The timing is irrelevant because DNI is taxed to the beneficiary regardless of whether distributions are actually made from a complex trust", "Distributing only $15,200 (the top of the trust''s lowest bracket) and retaining the rest minimizes total tax because it keeps the trust in a low bracket while minimizing the beneficiary''s additional income", "Distributing the full $200,000 before year-end is clearly preferable — the trust receives a $200,000 distribution deduction, eliminating its taxable income entirely, while James includes the $200,000 in his income but is taxed at graduated rates starting from his existing 22% bracket rather than the trust''s compressed 37% bracket, producing combined tax savings of approximately $25,000 or more compared to trust-level retention"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Trusts reach the highest marginal tax rate of 37% at just $15,200 of income, while individuals reach that rate at $609,350 (single) or $731,200 (married filing jointly). With $200,000 of DNI, the trust would pay approximately $67,000 in tax if the income is retained (most taxed at 37%). If the trustee distributes the full $200,000, the trust claims a distribution deduction under Section 661, reducing its taxable income to zero. James includes the $200,000 under Section 662 but is taxed at graduated individual rates — starting at 22% on income above $60,000 and reaching 32% at the upper end (total approximately $42,000). The distribution strategy saves approximately $25,000 in combined taxes. (A) is wrong because the compressed trust brackets produce far higher effective tax rates than individual brackets at these income levels. (B) is wrong because complex trusts are taxed on retained income — distributions must actually be made to shift the tax burden. (C) is wrong because retaining $184,800 in the trust at 37% produces far more tax than distributing it to a beneficiary in a lower bracket.',
  correct_index = 3,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1010;

-- ID 1011: Recommend trust structure to minimize impact of compressed brackets
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is counseling the Mercer family, which expects to receive $600,000 annually from a family business sale. The family is deciding between placing the funds in (1) a single irrevocable trust for three children, (2) three separate irrevocable trusts (one per child), or (3) distributing income directly to the children with no trust. Each child earns approximately $50,000 annually. The advisor must consider the trust compressed tax brackets, the kiddie tax rules (children are over age 24), and the family''s desire for asset protection. Recommend the most appropriate structure.',
  choices = '["Three separate trusts are optimal because each trust has its own set of compressed brackets — distributing $200,000 of income through each trust to each child produces three distribution deductions and taxes the income at individual rates, while providing the asset protection the family wants that direct distribution does not offer", "A single trust is best because it consolidates administrative costs and the $600,000 is taxed once at the trust level regardless of how many trusts are created", "Direct distribution to the children is always preferable because it avoids trust-level taxation entirely and the children''s low tax brackets minimize the total tax burden", "Three separate trusts provide no benefit over a single trust because the IRS treats related trusts as a single taxpayer under the grantor trust consolidation rules"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): The optimal structure balances tax efficiency with asset protection. Three separate trusts, each distributing $200,000 to its respective beneficiary, allow each trust to claim a distribution deduction under Section 661, passing the income to the beneficiaries at their individual tax rates. Each child includes $200,000 in income, taxed at graduated rates starting from their $50,000 base — significantly lower than the trust''s compressed brackets where the 37% rate begins at $15,200. Multiple trusts also provide flexibility: if one child needs less, that trust can retain more income and pay trust-level tax on a smaller amount. Direct distribution (C) achieves the same income tax result but sacrifices asset protection — creditors, divorcing spouses, and lawsuits can reach the funds. A single trust (B) produces the same tax result if it distributes all income, but offers less flexibility in allocation and less asset protection segregation per child. (D) is wrong because the IRS consolidation rules apply to substantially identical trusts created primarily for tax avoidance, not to trusts with separate beneficiaries and legitimate non-tax purposes.',
  correct_index = 0,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1011;

-- ID 1012: Assess whether distributing income to beneficiaries is advisable given beneficiary tax brackets
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is assessing the Wexford Estate, which has $150,000 of income in its final year. The estate''s two beneficiaries are (1) Patricia, a retired teacher with $25,000 of annual income in the 12% bracket, and (2) Victor, a surgeon with $500,000 of annual income in the 37% bracket. The executor has discretion to distribute income disproportionately. Should the executor distribute all income to Patricia, split it equally, or retain it in the estate?',
  choices = '["Retain all income in the estate because estate tax rates are lower than individual rates for high-income beneficiaries like Victor", "Distribute all $150,000 to Patricia — her 12% starting bracket means the first $75,000 of additional income is taxed at 12-22%, far below the estate''s compressed 37% rate on income over $15,200, saving approximately $25,000 or more in total tax compared to estate-level retention or equal distribution that would push half into Victor''s 37% bracket", "Split the income equally because the executor has a fiduciary duty to treat beneficiaries proportionally regardless of tax considerations", "Distribute all income to Victor because his higher bracket allows him to offset the income against business deductions unavailable to Patricia"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The estate reaches the 37% tax bracket at $15,200 of income, so retaining $150,000 would produce approximately $51,000 in estate-level tax. Distributing to Victor at 37% would save nothing — his marginal rate equals the estate''s rate on most of the income. Distributing all $150,000 to Patricia layers the income onto her $25,000 base: the first $19,400 addition is taxed at 12%, the next approximately $63,000 at 22%, and the remainder at 24% — a blended rate far below 37%. The estate claims a $150,000 distribution deduction under Section 661, zeroing out its taxable income. The total tax savings from directing income to Patricia versus retention is approximately $25,000+. Equal distribution wastes $75,000 on Victor at 37%. The executor''s fiduciary duty allows tax-efficient distributions when the trust instrument grants discretion, and tax minimization benefits all beneficiaries through greater total after-tax wealth. (A) is wrong because the estate''s compressed brackets produce higher tax than Patricia''s individual rates. (C) is wrong because fiduciary duty does not require equal distribution when the instrument grants discretion. (D) is wrong because there is no indication Victor has offsetting deductions.',
  correct_index = 1,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1012;

-- ============================================================
-- OWNER-ENTITY TRANSACTIONS (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 985: Evaluate whether redeeming shares from a related-party group achieves capital gain treatment
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is evaluating a proposed stock redemption for Hargrove Corp. Marcus owns 60% and his daughter Elena owns 40%. Marcus wants the corporation to redeem all of his shares for $500,000 (basis $100,000). Under Section 318, Marcus is deemed to constructively own Elena''s 40%, giving him 100% ownership both before and after the redemption of his directly owned shares. The advisor must determine whether this redemption qualifies for capital gain treatment or will be recharacterized as a dividend. Evaluate whether Marcus should proceed with this redemption structure.',
  choices = '["The redemption qualifies as a complete termination under Section 302(b)(3) because Marcus is surrendering all of his directly owned shares, which is sufficient regardless of constructive ownership", "The redemption automatically qualifies for capital gain treatment because any redemption that reduces a shareholder''s ownership percentage is treated as an exchange under Section 302(b)(2)", "Marcus should proceed because constructive ownership rules under Section 318 apply only to partnerships and trusts, not to family members in a corporate redemption context", "Marcus should not proceed with a simple redemption — the Section 318 family attribution rules cause Marcus to constructively own Elena''s 40% even after the redemption, preventing qualification under Section 302(b)(3) unless Marcus files a waiver of family attribution under Section 302(c)(2), agreeing to have no interest in the corporation (other than as a creditor) for 10 years, which converts the redemption to capital gain treatment on the $400,000 gain rather than dividend treatment on the full $500,000"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under Section 302(b)(3), a redemption qualifies for exchange (capital gain) treatment if it constitutes a complete termination of the shareholder''s interest. However, Section 318(a)(1) attributes stock owned by family members (spouse, children, grandchildren, parents) to the taxpayer. Marcus constructively owns Elena''s 40%, so after the redemption of his direct shares, he is treated as still owning 40% — not a complete termination. Without relief, the $500,000 is treated as a distribution under Section 301, taxed as a dividend to the extent of E&P ($500,000 at qualified dividend rates, with no offset for basis). However, Section 302(c)(2) allows Marcus to waive family attribution if he (1) has no interest in the corporation other than as a creditor for 10 years, (2) files a written agreement with the IRS, and (3) did not acquire any of the redeemed stock from a related person within the prior 10 years for tax avoidance purposes. With the waiver, the redemption qualifies under Section 302(b)(3), and Marcus recognizes $400,000 of capital gain ($500,000 minus $100,000 basis). (A) is wrong because constructive ownership must be considered. (B) is wrong because the substantially disproportionate test also requires consideration of constructive ownership. (C) is wrong because Section 318 explicitly applies to family members.',
  correct_index = 3,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 985;

-- ID 987: Recommend whether client should contribute appreciated or depreciated property to corporation
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is advising Thornfield, who owns two properties and wants to contribute one to his wholly owned C corporation. Property A has a basis of $50,000 and FMV of $200,000 (built-in gain of $150,000). Property B has a basis of $300,000 and FMV of $200,000 (built-in loss of $100,000). The corporation will use whichever property it receives in its business operations. Thornfield will retain the other property for personal investment. Recommend which property Thornfield should contribute to the corporation.',
  choices = '["Contribute Property A (built-in gain) to the corporation — under Section 351, the contribution is tax-free, the corporation takes a carryover basis of $50,000, and the built-in gain is deferred; Thornfield should then sell Property B personally to recognize the $100,000 capital loss on his individual return, because if he contributed Property B instead, the loss would be permanently trapped inside the corporation under Section 267 and Section 362(e)(2) basis limitation rules", "Contribute Property B (built-in loss) because the corporation receives a higher basis of $300,000, providing greater depreciation deductions at the corporate level", "Contribute Property B because Section 351 eliminates the built-in loss entirely, giving Thornfield a fresh start", "It makes no difference which property is contributed because Section 351 produces identical tax results regardless of whether the contributed property has built-in gain or built-in loss"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): This requires evaluating the asymmetric treatment of built-in gains and losses in Section 351 transfers. Contributing Property A (built-in gain) under Section 351 is tax-free to Thornfield and the corporation takes a $50,000 carryover basis under Section 362(a). The $150,000 built-in gain is deferred, not eliminated — it will be recognized when the corporation sells the property. Thornfield can then sell Property B personally for $200,000, recognizing a $100,000 capital loss deductible on his individual return. If Thornfield instead contributed Property B, Section 362(e)(2) would reduce the corporation''s basis from $300,000 to $200,000 (FMV) to prevent the importation of built-in losses into the corporation, destroying the $100,000 loss entirely. Alternatively, under Section 362(e)(2)(C), Thornfield could elect to reduce his stock basis instead, but the loss remains trapped at the shareholder level in reduced stock basis rather than being currently deductible. (B) is wrong because Section 362(e)(2) reduces the corporate basis to FMV, negating the higher-basis benefit. (C) is wrong about the mechanism but correctly identifies that the loss is effectively eliminated. (D) is wrong because built-in gains and losses receive materially different treatment.',
  correct_index = 0,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 987;

-- ============================================================
-- NONTAXABLE DISPOSITIONS (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 1236: Evaluate whether completing a like-kind exchange or taking the boot is more tax-efficient
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is evaluating options for Ridgemont Properties, which owns a commercial warehouse (basis $400,000, FMV $1,000,000). Ridgemont has identified a replacement property worth $850,000 and would receive $150,000 in cash boot to equalize values in a Section 1031 exchange. Alternatively, Ridgemont could sell the warehouse outright for $1,000,000 and purchase the replacement property separately. The combined federal and state capital gains rate is 28%. Evaluate which approach is more tax-efficient.',
  choices = '["The outright sale is preferable because Ridgemont can time the sale and purchase independently and the capital gains tax on $600,000 of gain is a one-time cost", "The two approaches produce identical tax results because the boot received in the exchange triggers the same total gain as an outright sale", "The exchange should be rejected entirely because any boot received disqualifies the transaction from Section 1031 treatment", "Completing the Section 1031 exchange with $150,000 boot is more tax-efficient — Ridgemont recognizes gain only to the extent of boot received ($150,000), deferring $450,000 of the $600,000 total gain and saving approximately $126,000 in current taxes compared to an outright sale that would trigger the full $600,000 gain, even though the replacement property takes a lower basis"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under Section 1031, gain is recognized only to the extent of boot received. In the exchange, Ridgemont''s realized gain is $600,000 ($1,000,000 FMV minus $400,000 basis), but recognized gain is limited to the $150,000 boot. The remaining $450,000 of gain is deferred through a reduced basis in the replacement property ($400,000 original basis minus $0 additional boot paid plus $150,000 gain recognized = $550,000 replacement basis, versus $850,000 FMV). The current tax on $150,000 at 28% is $42,000. An outright sale triggers $600,000 of gain and $168,000 in tax — a difference of $126,000 in current-year tax savings. Although the deferred gain reduces future depreciation deductions and increases gain on eventual disposition, the time value of deferring $126,000 in tax is substantial. (A) is wrong because the $126,000 tax deferral has significant present value even considering the eventual recognition of deferred gain. (B) is wrong because the boot limits current recognition to $150,000, not $600,000. (C) is wrong because receiving boot does not disqualify the exchange — it merely triggers partial gain recognition.',
  correct_index = 3,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1236;

-- ID 1238: Assess whether installment method or immediate recognition produces better after-tax results
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is assessing options for Wexford, who is selling investment land (basis $200,000) to an unrelated buyer for $800,000. The buyer will pay $200,000 at closing and $600,000 over six years with 5% interest. Wexford''s current marginal rate is 23.8% (20% capital gains plus 3.8% NIIT). Wexford expects his marginal rate to increase to 28.8% in three years due to anticipated tax legislation. The CPA is deciding between reporting on the installment method under Section 453 or electing out to recognize the full $600,000 gain in the current year. Assess which method produces better after-tax results.',
  choices = '["The installment method is always preferable because spreading gain over six years keeps Wexford in a lower bracket each year", "The installment method and immediate recognition produce identical total tax because the gross profit ratio ensures the same effective rate regardless of timing", "The installment method is preferable because the 5% interest income earned on deferred payments fully compensates for any rate increase risk", "Electing out and recognizing the full $600,000 gain in the current year at 23.8% is advisable — the total current tax is $142,800, whereas the installment method would subject later payments to a 28.8% rate, increasing total tax on the same $600,000 gain by approximately $30,000, and the installment method also carries the risk of further rate increases and the interest charge on deferred tax for high-income taxpayers under Section 453A"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Evaluating the installment method requires comparing current rates to expected future rates. The gross profit ratio is 75% ($600,000 gain divided by $800,000 selling price). Under the installment method, each payment is 75% gain: the $200,000 down payment triggers $150,000 of gain in year one at 23.8% ($35,700 tax), and the $600,000 in later payments triggers $450,000 of gain. If rates rise to 28.8%, the tax on the deferred gain increases by 5 percentage points — approximately $22,500 more on $450,000 of gain recognized in years with the higher rate. Additionally, Section 453A imposes an interest charge on the deferred tax liability for installment sales exceeding $5 million (or on the portion of deferred payments exceeding $5 million), though this threshold may not apply here. The election to recognize $600,000 of gain at 23.8% locks in $142,800 of tax with certainty and eliminates exposure to rate increases. (A) is wrong because the installment method does not reduce total gain — it merely spreads recognition, which is disadvantageous when rates are rising. (B) is wrong because different tax rates in different years produce different total tax. (C) is wrong because the interest income is separately taxable and does not offset higher capital gains rates.',
  correct_index = 3,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1238;

-- ============================================================
-- INDIVIDUAL TAX PLANNING: COMPENSATION (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 900: Evaluate optimal exercise timing considering AMT and regular tax implications
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is evaluating the exercise timing of incentive stock options (ISOs) held by Caldwell, a senior executive. Caldwell holds ISOs for 10,000 shares with an exercise price of $20 and current FMV of $70 per share. The $500,000 spread ($50 per share) is not taxable for regular tax purposes on exercise but is an AMT adjustment under Section 56. Caldwell''s regular tax liability is $180,000 and her tentative minimum tax (before the ISO exercise) is $170,000. If she exercises all options this year, the additional $500,000 AMT adjustment would push her tentative minimum tax well above her regular tax, triggering substantial AMT. Should the advisor recommend exercising all options this year or splitting the exercise across two years?',
  choices = '["Exercise all options this year because ISOs are never subject to tax until the shares are sold, making the timing of exercise irrelevant", "Split the exercise across two years — exercising 5,000 shares this year creates a $250,000 AMT adjustment that may keep the tentative minimum tax closer to the regular tax liability, minimizing or eliminating AMT in each year, whereas exercising all 10,000 shares creates a $500,000 adjustment that generates significant AMT above the regular tax threshold in a single year, and the AMT credit carryforward under Section 53 may take years to recover", "Exercise all options this year because the AMT credit carryforward under Section 53 guarantees full recovery of any AMT paid, making the timing economically neutral", "Delay all exercises until Caldwell leaves the company because ISOs can only be exercised tax-free after termination of employment"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): ISOs create an AMT adjustment equal to the spread on exercise (FMV minus exercise price) under Section 56(b)(3). With Caldwell''s tentative minimum tax already at $170,000 versus regular tax of $180,000, there is only $10,000 of headroom before AMT is triggered. Exercising all 10,000 shares adds $500,000 to AMTI, almost certainly pushing the tentative minimum tax far above regular tax and generating hundreds of thousands in AMT. While Section 53 allows an AMT credit carryforward, recovery depends on future years where regular tax exceeds tentative minimum tax — which may take many years. Splitting the exercise across two years keeps the annual AMT adjustment at $250,000, potentially allowing each year''s tentative minimum tax to remain closer to the regular tax threshold and reducing or eliminating the total AMT paid. (A) is wrong because while ISOs are not taxed for regular tax purposes on exercise, they trigger AMT adjustments. (C) is wrong because the AMT credit recovery is not guaranteed to be timely — the time value of money makes front-loaded AMT payments economically costly. (D) is wrong because ISOs can be exercised during employment and there is no tax-free exercise after termination.',
  correct_index = 1,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 900;

-- ID 901: Recommend deferral strategy balancing current vs. future tax rate expectations
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is advising Prescott, age 52, a corporate executive earning $800,000 annually, on whether to defer $200,000 of compensation through a nonqualified deferred compensation (NQDC) plan under Section 409A. The deferral reduces current taxable income from $800,000 to $600,000. Prescott expects to receive the deferred amounts at retirement (age 65), when he projects annual income of $150,000 from other sources. Current top marginal rate is 37%; Prescott estimates retirement rates could be 37% or higher due to legislative changes. The deferred amounts earn a market rate of return but carry the risk of forfeiture if the employer becomes insolvent. Recommend the most appropriate deferral strategy.',
  choices = '["Defer the maximum amount because the drop from $800,000 current income to $150,000 retirement income guarantees a lower tax bracket on the deferred amounts", "Do not defer because the risk of employer insolvency makes NQDC plans fundamentally unsuitable for any executive", "Defer a moderate amount (approximately $100,000) rather than the full $200,000 — this balances the likely bracket reduction at retirement against the unsecured creditor risk and the possibility that future tax rates may rise, ensuring that some income is taxed at known current rates while still capturing a bracket arbitrage on the deferred portion", "Defer the full $200,000 and invest the tax savings in a taxable brokerage account to hedge against the insolvency risk"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Evaluating NQDC deferral requires weighing multiple trade-offs. The bracket arbitrage is real: $200,000 taxed at 37% now versus potentially 24-32% at retirement saves meaningful tax. However, three risks argue against maximum deferral: (1) NQDC plans are unsecured promises to pay — if the employer goes bankrupt, Prescott is a general unsecured creditor who may lose the entire deferred amount; (2) tax rates 13 years from now are uncertain and could rise above current levels, eliminating or reversing the bracket benefit; (3) under Section 409A, the deferral election is irrevocable once made, and timing of distributions is inflexible. A moderate deferral captures most of the bracket arbitrage while limiting the downside exposure. (A) is wrong because it ignores insolvency risk and tax rate uncertainty. (B) is wrong because it categorically rejects a tool that provides meaningful tax benefits when used prudently. (D) is wrong because investing tax savings in a brokerage account does not hedge against losing the deferred compensation itself in an insolvency.',
  correct_index = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 901;

-- ============================================================
-- ENTITY TAX COMPLIANCE: NOLs AND CONSOLIDATED RETURNS (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 955: Evaluate whether filing consolidated or separate returns produces lower combined tax
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is evaluating the filing strategy for Hargrove Industries, which owns 100% of two subsidiaries. Subsidiary A has $3,000,000 of income, and Subsidiary B has $1,200,000 of losses. Subsidiary A also sold inventory to Subsidiary B for $500,000 (cost basis $300,000), and the inventory remains unsold by Subsidiary B at year-end. If the group files a consolidated return, intercompany gains are deferred under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-13 until the property leaves the group. If they file separately, each entity reports independently. Evaluate whether the consolidated or separate return produces lower combined tax.',
  choices = '["Separate returns are preferable because each subsidiary can optimize its own deductions and credits without the complexity of consolidated return regulations", "The consolidated return is clearly preferable — the $1,200,000 loss from Subsidiary B offsets Subsidiary A''s income, and the $200,000 intercompany gain on the inventory sale is deferred, reducing current consolidated taxable income to $1,600,000 (A''s $3,000,000 minus B''s $1,200,000 minus $200,000 deferred gain) versus $3,200,000 in combined separate taxable income ($3,200,000 income from A including the intercompany gain, with B''s $1,200,000 loss usable only against B''s future income), saving approximately $336,000 in current-year tax", "Consolidated returns never provide a benefit because the SRLY rules prevent subsidiary losses from offsetting parent income", "The results are identical because intercompany transactions are eliminated on both consolidated and separate returns"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Filing a consolidated return provides two distinct advantages here. First, Subsidiary B''s $1,200,000 loss offsets Subsidiary A''s income dollar-for-dollar in the current year. On separate returns, B''s loss can only offset B''s own income (which is zero), creating a $1,200,000 NOL carryforward that provides no current benefit. Second, the $200,000 intercompany gain on the inventory sale ($500,000 minus $300,000 cost) is deferred under Treas. Reg. 1.1502-13 until the inventory leaves the group. On separate returns, Subsidiary A recognizes the full $200,000 gain currently. Consolidated taxable income: $3,000,000 minus $1,200,000 minus $200,000 = $1,600,000, producing $336,000 in tax at 21%. Separate combined income: A reports $3,200,000 ($3,000,000 plus $200,000 gain), B reports $0 (loss carried forward), total tax = $672,000. The consolidated return saves $336,000. (A) is wrong because the loss offset and gain deferral benefits far outweigh administrative complexity. (C) is wrong because SRLY rules apply only to losses incurred before a subsidiary joins the group. (D) is wrong because intercompany gains are only deferred on consolidated returns.',
  correct_index = 1,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 955;

-- ID 957: Assess whether an ownership change restructuring outweighs the NOL limitation risk
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is assessing a proposed acquisition of Thornfield Corp, which has $10,000,000 of NOL carryforwards. Bancroft Industries wants to acquire 60% of Thornfield''s stock for $12,000,000. This acquisition would trigger an ''ownership change'' under Section 382, imposing an annual limitation on Thornfield''s NOL usage equal to the value of the loss corporation multiplied by the long-term tax-exempt rate (assume 4%). The annual Section 382 limitation would be approximately $800,000 ($20,000,000 total value times 4%). Without the acquisition, Thornfield projects $2,500,000 of annual taxable income that could absorb the NOLs in 4 years. Assess whether proceeding with the acquisition is advisable given the NOL limitation.',
  choices = '["The acquisition is inadvisable because Section 382 limits annual NOL usage to $800,000, meaning it would take over 12 years to fully utilize the $10,000,000 of NOLs compared to 4 years without the ownership change — the time value of the delayed tax deductions significantly reduces the present value of the NOLs", "The acquisition is advisable because Section 382 only limits the carryforward period, not the annual deduction amount, so the NOLs remain fully available", "The acquisition should proceed because the Section 382 limitation applies only to the acquiring corporation''s income, not to the loss corporation''s own income", "The NOL limitation is irrelevant because Section 382 was repealed by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act for ownership changes occurring after 2017"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Under Section 382, an ownership change imposes an annual limitation on the loss corporation''s ability to use pre-change NOLs. The limitation equals the value of the loss corporation immediately before the ownership change multiplied by the long-term tax-exempt rate. At $20,000,000 value and 4% rate, the annual limit is $800,000. Without the acquisition, Thornfield could use $2,500,000 per year against its own income, fully utilizing the NOLs in approximately 4 years. With the Section 382 limitation, utilization is capped at $800,000 annually, stretching full utilization to over 12 years. Using a 5% discount rate, the present value of $10,000,000 in NOL deductions is significantly lower when spread over 12+ years versus 4 years. Bancroft''s acquisition price should reflect this reduced NOL value. (B) is wrong because Section 382 limits the annual deduction amount, not just the carryforward period. (C) is wrong because Section 382 limits the loss corporation''s own use of pre-change NOLs regardless of the income source. (D) is wrong because Section 382 remains in full effect; the TCJA modified the NOL carryforward rules but did not repeal Section 382.',
  correct_index = 0,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 957;

