-- ID 994: Evaluate whether contributing high-basis or high-FMV property to partnership produces better tax results
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is evaluating partnership contribution strategies for Ashworth, who is forming a 50/50 partnership with a colleague. Ashworth can contribute either Property X (basis $400,000, FMV $200,000, built-in loss of $200,000) or Property Y (basis $50,000, FMV $200,000, built-in gain of $150,000). Both properties have the same FMV and would give Ashworth the same 50% partnership interest. The partnership will use the contributed property in business operations and expects to depreciate it. Evaluate which contribution produces better tax results for Ashworth.',
  choices = '["Contributing Property X (high basis, built-in loss) is preferable because the partnership receives a $400,000 basis, providing double the depreciation deductions and benefiting both partners equally", "Both contributions produce identical results because the partnership''s inside basis automatically adjusts to FMV under Section 723", "Contributing Property Y (built-in gain) is preferable — under Section 704(c), the $150,000 built-in gain is allocated entirely back to Ashworth on disposition, but this is deferred until sale, and Ashworth''s outside basis of $50,000 is low but adequate; contributing Property X is worse because Section 704(c) allocates the $200,000 built-in loss entirely to Ashworth, and Section 704(c) anti-abuse rules may limit the allocation of tax depreciation, preventing the high basis from benefiting the other partner", "Contributing Property X is preferable because Ashworth can sell it to the partnership at a loss and recognize the $200,000 loss immediately"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under Section 723, the partnership takes a carryover basis in contributed property, and Section 704(c) requires that the built-in gain or loss at contribution be allocated to the contributing partner. With Property Y, Ashworth''s outside basis is $50,000 under Section 722, and the partnership''s inside basis is $50,000 — the $150,000 built-in gain is allocated to Ashworth when the property is sold, but this is deferred. With Property X, the partnership''s inside basis is $400,000, but Section 704(c) requires the $200,000 built-in loss to be allocated to Ashworth. More importantly, the tax depreciation deductions may not benefit the non-contributing partner as expected because the ceiling rule under the traditional method of Section 704(c) limits the other partner''s depreciation to book depreciation (based on $200,000 FMV), not the higher tax basis. Ashworth would be better served by selling Property X on the open market to recognize the $200,000 loss personally, then contributing the cash proceeds. (A) is wrong because Section 704(c) prevents the higher basis from proportionally benefiting the other partner. (B) is wrong because Section 723 uses carryover basis, not FMV. (D) is wrong because selling to a 50%-owned partnership would trigger related-party rules under Section 707(b).',
  correct_index = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 994;

-- ID 995: Recommend allocation method (traditional, remedial, curative) that best achieves partner goals
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is recommending a Section 704(c) allocation method for Mercer Partners LLC. Partner A contributed property with a basis of $100,000 and FMV of $500,000. Partner B contributed $500,000 cash. The property is depreciable over 10 years. Under the traditional method, the ceiling rule limits total depreciation to the $100,000 tax basis ($10,000 per year), meaning Partner B receives only $10,000 per year in tax depreciation instead of the $25,000 book depreciation. Under the remedial method, the partnership creates remedial allocations to give Partner B the full $25,000 of tax depreciation annually while allocating an offsetting $15,000 of income to Partner A. Which method should the advisor recommend if Partner B''s primary goal is maximizing current-year tax deductions?',
  choices = '["The traditional method is best for all partners because it is simplest and the ceiling rule protects Partner A from excess income allocations", "The curative method is optimal because it uses other partnership tax items to correct the ceiling rule distortion without creating fictional remedial items", "The remedial method best achieves Partner B''s goal — it guarantees Partner B receives the full $25,000 annual tax depreciation that matches book depreciation, eliminating the ceiling rule shortfall entirely, even though Partner A absorbs $15,000 of offsetting remedial income per year; Partner A''s additional income is the cost of ensuring the partnership agreement accurately reflects the economic deal where both partners contributed equal value", "No allocation method matters because Section 704(c) applies only at the time of contribution, not to ongoing depreciation"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under Treas. Reg. 1.704-3, three methods address Section 704(c) built-in gain or loss allocations. The traditional method applies the ceiling rule, which limits total tax depreciation to the $100,000 tax basis ($10,000 per year), leaving Partner B with only $10,000 of tax depreciation instead of the economically accurate $25,000. The curative method can use other partnership income or gain items to compensate Partner B for the ceiling rule shortfall, but only if such items exist — it depends on the partnership having sufficient offsetting items. The remedial method creates notional tax items specifically to eliminate the ceiling rule distortion: Partner B receives a remedial depreciation deduction of $15,000 per year (bringing total to $25,000), and Partner A is allocated $15,000 of remedial income. This guarantees Partner B receives the intended economic benefit regardless of other partnership operations. For Partner B''s goal of maximizing tax deductions, the remedial method is the only method that guarantees full correction. (A) protects Partner A but disadvantages Partner B. (B) works only when sufficient offsetting items exist. (D) is wrong because Section 704(c) affects the allocation of depreciation throughout the property''s life.',
  correct_index = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 995;

