-- ID 1036: Evaluate which entity type best serves a startup with multiple investors seeking pass-through losses
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is evaluating entity selection for a new technology startup. The founders expect $800,000 in losses during the first three years, followed by significant profitability. There are 8 individual investors (all U.S. citizens) who want to deduct their share of startup losses against other income. The investors also want the ability to add a venture capital fund (a partnership) as a future investor. The advisor is comparing a C corporation, an S corporation, and a partnership (LLC taxed as a partnership). Evaluate which entity type best serves the investors'' goals.',
  choices = '["The C corporation is best because startup losses create NOL carryforwards that provide greater long-term value than pass-through deductions", "The S corporation is optimal because it provides pass-through treatment for the $800,000 in losses while offering limited liability and avoiding self-employment tax on distributed profits", "The LLC taxed as a partnership best achieves the investors'' goals — it passes through losses to the 8 investors for deduction against other income (subject to basis, at-risk, and passive activity limitations), and unlike an S corporation, it can admit the venture capital fund (a partnership) as a future member without violating entity eligibility rules, since S corporations cannot have partnership or entity shareholders", "All three entities produce identical tax results because the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act equalized the treatment of business losses across entity types"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): The investors'' goals have two components: (1) pass-through losses in early years and (2) flexibility to admit a partnership-type investor. A C corporation fails goal (1) because losses are trapped at the entity level and cannot pass through to shareholders. An S corporation provides pass-through losses but fails goal (2) because Section 1361(b)(1)(B) prohibits partnerships, corporations, and most entities from being S corporation shareholders — admitting the venture capital fund would terminate the S election. The LLC taxed as a partnership satisfies both goals: losses flow through to members under Section 702, and there are no restrictions on the type of entity that can be a partner. The losses are subject to the individual partners'' basis limitations (Section 704(d)), at-risk rules (Section 465), and passive activity rules (Section 469), but the investors can structure their participation to maximize deductibility. (A) is wrong because the investors specifically want current pass-through deductions. (B) is wrong because the S corporation cannot accommodate the venture capital fund. (D) is wrong because entity type fundamentally affects loss treatment.',
  correct_index = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1036;

-- ID 1037: Assess whether increasing salary vs. distribution split reduces total tax burden
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is assessing the compensation structure for Dr. Hargrove, the sole shareholder of an S corporation medical practice earning $500,000 of net income. Currently, Dr. Hargrove takes $200,000 as salary (subject to FICA: 15.3% on the first $160,200 and 2.9% Medicare above that) and $300,000 as distributions (not subject to FICA). The IRS is known to challenge S corporation owners who set unreasonably low salaries to avoid employment taxes. A colleague suggests increasing the salary to $350,000 to avoid IRS scrutiny. Assess whether increasing the salary to $350,000 is advisable.',
  choices = '["Increase to $350,000 because the IRS requires S corporation owners to take at least 70% of net income as salary", "Maintain the $200,000 salary — increasing to $350,000 adds $150,000 to the wage base, generating approximately $4,350 in additional Medicare tax (2.9% of $150,000) with no offsetting tax benefit, and $200,000 is defensible as reasonable compensation for a physician if it aligns with comparable market compensation for similar services in the same geographic area", "Increase to $350,000 because the additional FICA taxes are fully deductible and produce a net tax savings", "Set the salary to the minimum wage because S corporation distributions are always more tax-efficient than wages"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The ''reasonable compensation'' standard under Section 162 and IRS guidance requires S corporation shareholder-employees to receive compensation commensurate with the services they provide — not a fixed percentage of income. Increasing from $200,000 to $350,000 subjects an additional $150,000 to the 2.9% Medicare tax (both employee and employer shares), costing approximately $4,350 in additional payroll taxes with no corresponding tax benefit. The key question is whether $200,000 is defensible as ''reasonable'' for Dr. Hargrove''s services. If comparable physicians in the same specialty and market earn $200,000-$250,000, then $200,000 is reasonable and the $300,000 distribution is not disguised compensation. Increasing salary beyond what is necessary to satisfy the reasonable compensation standard wastes money on unnecessary employment taxes. (A) is wrong because there is no 70% rule — the standard is reasonable compensation based on market comparables. (C) is wrong because while the employer share of FICA is deductible, the deduction does not offset the full cost of additional employment taxes. (D) is wrong because unreasonably low salaries invite IRS reclassification of distributions as wages with penalties.',
  correct_index = 1,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1037;

