-- CAPITAL STRUCTURE TAX PLANNING (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 1227: Evaluate whether making an ATC election is beneficial for a capital-intensive business
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is evaluating the Section 163(j) interest limitation for Ridgemont Manufacturing, a capital-intensive business with $8,000,000 of adjusted taxable income, $3,500,000 of business interest expense, and $4,000,000 of annual depreciation deductions. Under current rules (post-2021), depreciation is not added back to ATI, so the Section 163(j) limit is $2,400,000 (30% of $8,000,000), leaving $1,100,000 of disallowed interest. Ridgemont is considering making an electing real property trade or business (EPTB) or electing farming business election under the applicable alternative tax capacity (ATC) rules. However, electing out of Section 163(j) requires using the alternative depreciation system (ADS) for real and certain other property, which extends useful lives and eliminates bonus depreciation. Evaluate whether the election is beneficial.',
  choices = '["The election is not beneficial for Ridgemont — the $1,100,000 of currently disallowed interest (which carries forward indefinitely) is less valuable than the $4,000,000 of accelerated depreciation that would be lost by switching to ADS, because ADS extends useful lives and eliminates bonus depreciation, reducing current-year depreciation significantly and increasing taxable income by more than the interest deduction gained", "The election is always beneficial because eliminating the interest limitation allows the full $3,500,000 deduction, which exceeds any depreciation reduction", "The election has no effect because manufacturing companies are automatically exempt from Section 163(j) as qualifying trades or businesses", "The election should be made because the disallowed interest carryforward expires after five years and will be permanently lost if not elected out"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (A): Evaluating the election requires comparing the marginal benefit of deducting $1,100,000 of currently disallowed interest against the cost of losing accelerated depreciation on $4,000,000 of annual depreciation. Under ADS, useful lives are extended (e.g., nonresidential real property from 39 to 40 years, certain equipment to longer lives) and bonus depreciation is eliminated. For a capital-intensive manufacturer with $4,000,000 in annual depreciation, the reduction in current-year depreciation from switching to ADS could easily exceed $1,000,000 or more, offsetting or exceeding the interest deduction gained. Furthermore, the disallowed interest under Section 163(j) carries forward indefinitely under current law, so it is not permanently lost — it can be deducted in future years when ATI is higher or interest expense is lower. The election, once made, is irrevocable, locking Ridgemont into slower depreciation permanently. (B) is wrong because it ignores the depreciation cost of the election. (C) is wrong because manufacturing companies are not automatically exempt from Section 163(j). (D) is wrong because disallowed business interest carries forward indefinitely, not five years.',
  correct_index = 0,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1227;

-- ID 1228: Recommend whether structuring to stay below the gross receipts threshold is advisable
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is advising Thornfield Services, which has average annual gross receipts of $28,000,000 over the prior three years. The Section 163(j) small business exception exempts taxpayers with three-year average gross receipts of $29,000,000 or less (2024, indexed for inflation). Thornfield projects $32,000,000 in gross receipts for the current year, which would push the three-year average above the threshold. Thornfield''s annual interest expense is $1,500,000 and ATI is $4,000,000. The CPA is considering whether Thornfield should defer $4,000,000 of revenue recognition to the following year to stay below the threshold. Evaluate whether this strategy is advisable.',
  choices = '["The strategy is advisable because staying below the $29,000,000 threshold exempts the full $1,500,000 interest deduction, saving $315,000 in tax (21% of $1,500,000) that would otherwise be partially disallowed", "The strategy is unnecessary because the small business exception applies on a per-entity basis and Thornfield can split into two entities to maintain each below the threshold", "The strategy is not advisable — deferring $4,000,000 of revenue to avoid the Section 163(j) limitation saves at most $210,000 in current-year tax (the disallowed interest of $300,000 times 21%), while deferring $4,000,000 of revenue costs $840,000 in deferred tax benefit (21% of $4,000,000), and aggressive revenue deferral may violate the taxpayer''s accounting method or trigger IRS scrutiny for lacking economic substance", "Revenue deferral has no effect on the gross receipts test because the threshold is based on cash receipts, not accrual-method revenue"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Without the small business exception, Thornfield''s Section 163(j) limit is 30% of ATI: 30% of $4,000,000 = $1,200,000. The disallowed interest is $300,000 ($1,500,000 minus $1,200,000), costing $63,000 in current tax (21% of $300,000) — though this amount carries forward indefinitely. The proposed deferral of $4,000,000 of revenue delays $840,000 of tax (21% of $4,000,000) but the revenue must still be recognized eventually. The one-time interest deduction benefit of avoiding $300,000 of disallowed interest (worth $63,000 in tax) is dwarfed by the economic and compliance costs of deferring $4,000,000 of revenue. Additionally, artificial revenue deferral without a legitimate business purpose may violate the taxpayer''s established accounting method and could be challenged under the economic substance doctrine or Section 446 change-in-method rules. (A) overstates the benefit — the disallowed interest is $300,000 (not $1,500,000), and it carries forward. (B) is wrong because the aggregation rules under Section 448(c)(2) combine related entities. (D) is wrong because the gross receipts test under Section 448(c) applies to gross receipts as determined under the taxpayer''s method of accounting.',
  correct_index = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1228;

-- ============================================================
-- RELATED PARTY TRANSACTIONS (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 1246: Evaluate whether restructuring a sale to avoid related-party loss denial is worth the transaction costs
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is evaluating a proposed sale of equipment from Hargrove (an individual) to Hargrove Family LLC, in which Hargrove owns a 60% interest. The equipment has a basis of $300,000 and FMV of $180,000. Under Section 267(a)(1), losses on sales between related parties (including a more-than-50% owner and their entity) are disallowed. Hargrove is considering selling the equipment to an unrelated third party for $180,000 instead, recognizing the $120,000 loss, and having the LLC purchase similar equipment from the market for $180,000. The third-party sale would incur $15,000 in transaction costs (broker fees, transfer taxes). Evaluate whether the restructured transaction is worth the additional costs.',
  choices = '["The restructured sale is not worth it because the $15,000 transaction costs reduce the net loss to $105,000, which is less than the original $120,000 loss", "The restructured sale provides no benefit because Section 267 applies to any sale where the ultimate buyer is related to the seller, regardless of whether an intermediary is used", "The related-party sale is preferable because the disallowed loss transfers to the LLC and can be recognized when the LLC subsequently sells the equipment to a third party", "The restructured sale is advisable — recognizing a $120,000 capital loss on the third-party sale produces a tax benefit of approximately $28,560 (at 23.8%) after accounting for the $15,000 transaction costs (which increase the loss to $135,000 if deductible, or reduce proceeds to $165,000), netting approximately $13,560 in economic benefit compared to a related-party sale where the $120,000 loss is permanently disallowed under Section 267 and provides zero tax benefit to Hargrove"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): Under Section 267(a)(1), the $120,000 loss on a direct sale to the related LLC is completely disallowed — Hargrove receives no tax benefit. The related purchaser (the LLC) can use the disallowed loss only to reduce gain on a subsequent sale under Section 267(d), but cannot create or increase a loss. By selling to an unrelated party, Hargrove recognizes the full loss. The $15,000 in transaction costs reduce net proceeds to $165,000, increasing the realized loss to $135,000 ($300,000 basis minus $165,000 net proceeds). At a 23.8% combined capital gains rate, the tax benefit is approximately $32,130. The net economic benefit after transaction costs is approximately $17,130 ($32,130 tax savings minus the additional opportunity cost). This exceeds the zero benefit from the related-party sale. (A) is wrong because it compares the wrong numbers — the transaction costs do not reduce the tax benefit below zero; they slightly increase the deductible loss. (B) is wrong because Section 267 applies to direct sales between related parties, not to separate arm''s-length transactions with an unrelated buyer, and the step-transaction doctrine would not apply if the two transactions are genuinely independent. (C) is wrong because the disallowed loss does not transfer as a deductible loss to the LLC — it can only offset future gain.',
  correct_index = 3,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1246;

-- ID 1247: Assess whether transferring property through an intermediary to achieve loss recognition is advisable
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A CPA is assessing a proposed transaction where Wexford wants to sell stock (basis $500,000, FMV $300,000) to his brother Marcus. The $200,000 loss would be disallowed under Section 267 as a related-party sale. Wexford''s tax attorney suggests selling the stock to an unrelated friend, Patterson, who would then immediately resell the stock to Marcus at the same $300,000 price. The purpose is to recognize Wexford''s $200,000 loss on the sale to Patterson. Assess whether this intermediary strategy is advisable.',
  choices = '["The strategy is advisable because the sale to Patterson is a bona fide transaction with an unrelated party, and Section 267 does not apply to the first sale", "The strategy is inadvisable — the IRS would likely collapse the two transactions under the step-transaction doctrine, treating the sale as effectively a direct transfer from Wexford to Marcus and disallowing the $200,000 loss under Section 267; the prearranged nature of the intermediary sale, the immediate resale at the same price, and the absence of economic risk to Patterson all indicate the intermediary has no independent economic purpose beyond tax avoidance", "The strategy is advisable because the step-transaction doctrine only applies to corporate reorganizations, not to individual property sales", "The strategy is unnecessary because Section 267 loss disallowance applies only to sales of depreciable property between related parties, not to stock sales"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): The step-transaction doctrine allows the IRS to collapse a series of formally separate transactions into a single integrated transaction when the steps are prearranged, interdependent, and lack independent economic significance apart from tax benefits. Here, the sale to Patterson followed by Patterson''s immediate resale to Marcus at the same price has the hallmarks of a prearranged conduit transaction: (1) the binding or mutual commitment between the parties suggests a predetermined outcome, (2) Patterson bears no economic risk as an intermediary buying and selling at the same price, and (3) the sole purpose is to avoid Section 267 loss disallowance. If collapsed, the transaction is treated as a direct sale from Wexford to Marcus, and the $200,000 loss is disallowed. Additionally, Wexford could face penalties for an undisclosed listed transaction or a substantial understatement of tax. (A) is wrong because it ignores the step-transaction doctrine. (C) is wrong because the step-transaction doctrine applies broadly across all areas of tax law. (D) is wrong because Section 267(a)(1) disallows losses on sales of any property (not just depreciable property) between related parties as defined in Section 267(b), and siblings are related parties under Section 267(b)(1).',
  correct_index = 1,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 1247;

