-- ENTITY FORMATION AND RESTRUCTURING (1 question)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 5490: Evaluate whether a Type A or Type C reorganization better achieves the client's goals
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'A tax advisor is evaluating acquisition structures for Bancroft Industries, which wants to acquire all of Thornfield Corp''s assets. Thornfield has assets worth $20,000,000 (basis $8,000,000) and $3,000,000 of liabilities. In a Type A statutory merger, Thornfield merges into Bancroft, and Thornfield''s shareholders receive Bancroft stock plus up to 40% cash consideration (boot). In a Type C ''practical merger,'' Bancroft acquires substantially all of Thornfield''s assets solely for voting stock (with a limited boot exception of up to 20% if the solely-for-voting-stock requirement is met for 80%). Thornfield''s shareholders prefer receiving some cash. Evaluate which reorganization type better achieves the combined goals of tax deferral for shareholders and cash distribution flexibility.',
  choices = '["Type C is preferable because it allows up to 20% boot, giving shareholders $4,000,000 in cash while deferring gain on the remaining $16,000,000 of stock consideration", "Both types produce identical results because the continuity-of-interest doctrine applies equally and imposes the same boot limitations on both Type A and Type C reorganizations", "Type C is preferable because it avoids the complexity of a statutory merger and achieves the same tax result with simpler documentation", "Type A is clearly preferable — it permits substantially more boot (cash or other property) to shareholders without jeopardizing the reorganization''s tax-free status, because Type A has no statutory boot limitation beyond the judicial continuity-of-interest requirement (generally 40-50% equity is sufficient), whereas Type C''s boot exception is severely restricted because assumed liabilities count as boot for purposes of the 20% test, and Thornfield''s $3,000,000 of liabilities alone may consume the entire boot allowance"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (D): The critical distinction is the boot flexibility. In a Type A reorganization, there is no statutory limit on boot — the only constraint is the judicial continuity-of-interest (COI) doctrine, which generally requires that a substantial portion (40-50% per IRS rulings) of the total consideration be acquirer stock. This allows Thornfield''s shareholders to receive significant cash while still qualifying for tax-free treatment on the stock portion. In a Type C reorganization, the ''solely for voting stock'' requirement under Section 368(a)(1)(C) is strict. While Section 368(a)(2)(B) permits boot of up to 20% of the total consideration, assumed liabilities are treated as boot for purposes of this test. Thornfield''s $3,000,000 of liabilities, when treated as boot, may consume most or all of the 20% allowance ($4,000,000 if total consideration is $20,000,000), leaving little or no room for cash to shareholders. Type A provides far greater flexibility to satisfy the shareholders'' preference for cash. (A) is wrong because the $3,000,000 of assumed liabilities counts against the 20% boot allowance in a Type C, severely limiting actual cash distributions. (B) is wrong because the COI doctrine creates a flexible standard for Type A while the statutory requirements for Type C are much more restrictive. (C) is wrong because Type C is not simpler and produces a materially different boot limitation.',
  correct_index = 3,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_level = 4
WHERE id = 5490;

COMMIT;
